WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.130
While seven hundred thousand people in the city were starving to death, tons of rice, maize and potatoes sat in the basement of a single building.

2
00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:14.500
The men who kept watch in that basement starved to death too — without laying a hand on the sacks.

3
00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:19.430
To them those seeds were not food; they were the bread of the world that would come after the war.

4
00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:35.600
While seven hundred thousand people in the city were starving to death, tons of rice, corn and potatoes sat in the basement of a single building.

5
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:41.810
The men who stood guard over that basement starved to death as well — without ever laying a hand on the sacks.

6
00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:47.700
To them those seeds were not food; they were the bread of the world that would come after the war.

7
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:50.230
Picture a library.

8
00:00:50.230 --> 00:00:55.960
It has shelves, it has catalogue cards, it has numbered boxes.

9
00:00:55.960 --> 00:00:59.450
But not one book in this library is made of paper.

10
00:00:59.450 --> 00:01:02.300
Each one is a handful of seed.

11
00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:08.830
Some of it wheat, some barley, some a potato tuber, some a mountain oat whose name you have never heard.

12
00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:19.360
On every box it is written where it came from: the Andean slopes of Peru, the highlands of Ethiopia, the dry valleys of Afghanistan, the rice terraces of Taiwan.

13
00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:22.850
And every book in this library is alive.

14
00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:26.340
Put it in the right soil and it is born again.

15
00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:30.310
The man who built this library was called Nikolai Vavilov.

16
00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:37.480
He was born in eighteen eighty-seven in Moscow, the son of a father who had been a village boy himself before becoming a merchant.

17
00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:42.730
The childhood stories his father told always came back to the same thing: famine.

18
00:01:42.730 --> 00:01:49.580
The famines of the Russian countryside that returned at intervals, emptied villages, drove people to eat grass.

19
00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:53.550
Vavilov gave his whole scientific life to a single question.

20
00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:57.040
What has to be done so that it never happens again?

21
00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:05.170
The answer lay somewhere that sounds entirely obvious to us today, but that nobody at the time was thinking about on this scale: in diversity.

22
00:02:05.170 --> 00:02:11.860
Vavilov knew that a single variety of wheat sown across a field was fragile, however productive it might be.

23
00:02:11.860 --> 00:02:18.550
A new disease, a new insect, a drought lasting a few years could wipe that variety off the face of the earth.

24
00:02:18.550 --> 00:02:27.160
But peasants all over the world had spent thousands of years sifting and selecting, keeping the varieties that endured in their own valleys, in their own climate.

25
00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:31.610
Somewhere out there was a rye that stood up to the frosts of Siberia.

26
00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:35.260
There was a barley that could take salted soil.

27
00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:38.110
There was a wheat that never caught rust.

28
00:02:38.110 --> 00:02:45.120
Find them, bring them together, and you would hold a gene pool capable of saving the agriculture of the future.

29
00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:50.210
What is more, Vavilov also described where these seeds were to be looked for.

30
00:02:50.210 --> 00:02:59.460
According to the theory he developed on the origin of cultivated plants, every cultivated plant had certain centres on the globe where its diversity was most concentrated;

31
00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:03.110
those were the places where that plant had been domesticated.

32
00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:13.160
To this he added an idea he called the law of homologous series: the tendency of related species to produce similar variations.

33
00:03:13.160 --> 00:03:16.810
This gave the botanist a kind of power of prophecy.

34
00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:23.820
You could say in advance that a trait you had seen in one species might be found somewhere in its sister species too.

35
00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:32.590
When he set out this idea at a congress in nineteen twenty, the reaction of the scientists in the hall was this: biology has finally found its own Mendeleev.

36
00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:36.560
And then he picked up his suitcase and went out to collect the world.

37
00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:45.330
He went to Iran, to Afghanistan, to Ethiopia, to Japan, to Korea, to Mexico, to Brazil, to Algeria.

38
00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:52.340
There were mountains he crossed on the back of a mule, borders he crossed at the risk of arrest, nights he spent shaking with malaria.

39
00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:59.670
Over the course of his life he went on a hundred and fifteen collecting expeditions and brought back material from sixty-four countries.

40
00:03:59.670 --> 00:04:11.320
Every time he returned the suitcases were full of seed; and on every seed packet, in that small, neat handwriting, was written where it had been taken, at what altitude it grew, what name the peasant had given it.

41
00:04:11.320 --> 00:04:17.690
This treasure was gathered in Leningrad, in an old ministry building looking out over Saint Isaac's Square.

42
00:04:17.690 --> 00:04:24.380
The institution bore the name All-Union Institute of Plant Breeding; let us simply call it the Plant Institute.

43
00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:35.550
At the far end of the square stood the golden dome of the cathedral; inside the building, in rooms running the length of the corridor, in wooden drawers, in tin boxes, in thick paper envelopes,

44
00:04:35.550 --> 00:04:38.880
sat the largest seed collection in the world.

45
00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:44.610
By nineteen forty the number of samples on the shelves had reached two hundred and fifty thousand.

46
00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:48.100
At that date there was nothing like it anywhere on earth.

47
00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:56.070
America had collecting programmes, so did Germany; but none of them was on this scale, this systematic, this meticulous.

48
00:04:56.070 --> 00:04:58.760
And it was not a museum.

49
00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:02.730
The institute was obliged to keep the collection alive.

50
00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:07.980
Seed does not live forever; within a few years it loses its power to germinate.

51
00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:14.030
So every sample had to be put in the soil at set intervals, harvested again, renewed.

52
00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:24.720
That was what the experimental stations set up across the country were for; the station at Pavlovsk, south of Leningrad, had been opened for exactly that purpose in nineteen twenty-six.

53
00:05:24.720 --> 00:05:31.570
Which means that those drawers in the building were in fact the heart of a giant, constantly turning agricultural machine.

54
00:05:31.570 --> 00:05:37.780
Dozens of botanists, agronomists and technicians had given their lives to a single group of plants.

55
00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:41.430
One concerned himself with nothing but oats.

56
00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:44.120
One with nothing but rice.

57
00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:46.810
One with nothing but groundnuts.

58
00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:57.340
One of them, the man who held tens of thousands of potato samples in his care, believed that the potato was the second thing after bread for humankind; because that was what Vavilov had told him.

59
00:05:57.340 --> 00:06:01.630
These people were the guardians of the largest seed collection in the world.

60
00:06:01.630 --> 00:06:06.400
A few years later that sentence would stop being a figure of speech.

61
00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:12.290
The cracking of the world Vavilov had built began not in the field, but at the lectern.

62
00:06:12.290 --> 00:06:18.820
In the late nineteen twenties a name rose in Soviet agricultural circles: Trofim Lysenko.

63
00:06:18.820 --> 00:06:26.310
He was an agronomist working at an experimental station in Azerbaijan, thin on academic training but bold in the field.

64
00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:33.480
His fame came from the claim that by soaking seed before sowing and holding it in the cold, winter wheat could be adapted to spring.

65
00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:39.530
The method itself was not new and its effect was limited; but Lysenko's presentation was new.

66
00:06:39.530 --> 00:06:44.460
He spoke not in the language of the laboratory but in the language of the peasant.

67
00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:51.150
He did not promise years of crossbreeding work; he promised harvests that would multiply within a few seasons.

68
00:06:51.150 --> 00:07:00.400
For an administration wrestling with the famine that had followed collectivization, an administration that wanted results, this was exactly the voice it had been waiting for.

69
00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:04.850
As Lysenko rose, his claims grew with him.

70
00:07:04.850 --> 00:07:12.340
He argued that acquired characteristics passed to later generations, that the environment could directly knead a plant's heredity.

71
00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:16.630
This ran against everything genetics had accumulated up to that day.

72
00:07:16.630 --> 00:07:21.240
But Lysenko did not conduct the argument on scientific ground.

73
00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:27.290
He declared genetics a bourgeois science: abstract, cut off from the people, foreign.

74
00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:31.420
He put not his opponents' ideas but their loyalty under question.

75
00:07:31.420 --> 00:07:34.590
Vavilov supported him at first.

76
00:07:34.590 --> 00:07:40.640
He thought it natural to open a door to a young, eager researcher; for a few years he gave his work room.

77
00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:47.490
But as Lysenko's claims stopped being testable and turned into a system of belief, Vavilov began to push back.

78
00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:52.420
At one plant breeding congress after another the two faced each other.

79
00:07:52.420 --> 00:07:58.470
Lysenko accused him of being a Mendelist-Morganist, that is, of serving an imported and harmful science.

80
00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:08.840
At a meeting in nineteen thirty-nine Vavilov spoke that famous line of his: we shall go to the pyre, we shall burn, but we shall not renounce what we believe.

81
00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:15.690
This was one of the most dangerous sentences that could be uttered in the Soviet Union in the year nineteen thirty-nine.

82
00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:20.300
The ring around the institute had already begun to tighten in those years.

83
00:08:20.300 --> 00:08:27.630
Some of Vavilov's close colleagues were arrested in the late nineteen thirties; some were sent to the camps, some never came back.

84
00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:38.480
Inside the institution a machinery of denunciation was at work; his expeditions abroad were spoken of as possible espionage, and his correspondence with foreign scientists went into the files.

85
00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:46.930
On the sixth of August, nineteen forty, Vavilov was doing fieldwork in the western territories the Soviet Union had newly annexed.

86
00:08:46.930 --> 00:08:50.100
A van caught up with him out in the open country.

87
00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:53.590
He was told there was urgent business in Moscow.

88
00:08:53.590 --> 00:09:00.120
When his companions came back to camp that evening, his bag and the plants collected that day were still lying there.

89
00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:05.690
His interrogation lasted eleven months and ran through more than four hundred sessions.

90
00:09:05.690 --> 00:09:14.940
The accusations packed into the file were built around sabotage against the country and collaboration with foreign intelligence; not one of them had any connection to the truth.

91
00:09:14.940 --> 00:09:19.230
In the summer of nineteen forty-one he was sentenced to death.

92
00:09:19.230 --> 00:09:22.880
The sentence was later commuted to twenty years' imprisonment.

93
00:09:22.880 --> 00:09:30.050
In the autumn of that same year, as the Germans drew closer to Moscow, he was transferred to the prison in Saratov.

94
00:09:30.050 --> 00:09:33.220
It was his cellmates who told the rest.

95
00:09:33.220 --> 00:09:39.270
In those cells, where nobody had anything left to say to anybody, Vavilov had begun to teach.

96
00:09:39.270 --> 00:09:44.360
Genetics, plant geography, the history of world agriculture.

97
00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:49.770
A course of more than a hundred hours, with no blackboard, no books, no paper.

98
00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:55.500
The man who had built the largest seed collection in the world could now pass on only what was in his memory.

99
00:09:55.500 --> 00:09:59.310
The building in Leningrad, meanwhile, had been left headless.

100
00:09:59.310 --> 00:10:09.680
Its founder's name could no longer be spoken; the logic by which he had arranged the shelves, which expedition corresponded to which drawer, why the collection mattered so much,

101
00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:13.490
now lived only in the heads of the people who worked there.

102
00:10:13.490 --> 00:10:21.940
And those people knew perfectly well what it meant to stand by that collection in a country where the founder of their own institution had been taken in as a traitor.

103
00:10:21.940 --> 00:10:26.070
A year later, the meaning of standing by it would change once more.

104
00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:32.120
On the twenty-second of June, nineteen forty-one, Germany attacked the Soviet Union.

105
00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:38.490
The armies on the northern front advanced quickly through the Baltic, and at the end of their route stood Leningrad.

106
00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:44.860
In the city the children were evacuated first, then factories began to be dismantled and carried east.

107
00:10:44.860 --> 00:10:50.590
The institute saw the danger too and applied for the collection to be taken out of the city.

108
00:10:50.590 --> 00:11:01.280
But in the first months of a war, on an evacuation list where tank factories and artillery ammunition were queueing up, hundreds of heavy crates full of seed were nobody's priority.

109
00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:04.130
The answer was slow in coming.

110
00:11:04.130 --> 00:11:07.460
Then there was no longer a door left to answer it.

111
00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:13.830
On the eighth of September, nineteen forty-one, German units cut the last land corridor south of Lake Ladoga.

112
00:11:13.830 --> 00:11:17.000
Every road into Leningrad was closed.

113
00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:24.490
Inside the city were more than two million people, the largest seed collection in the world, and a steadily shrinking stock of food.

114
00:11:24.490 --> 00:11:29.740
In those same days one of the city's main food depots caught fire in a bombardment.

115
00:11:29.740 --> 00:11:35.150
Flour, sugar and oil burned in the blaze; the melting sugar soaked into the ground.

116
00:11:35.150 --> 00:11:41.360
The following winter people would dig up that earth, fill jars with it and sell it as sugared mud.

117
00:11:41.360 --> 00:11:44.530
The bread ration melted away within weeks.

118
00:11:44.530 --> 00:11:51.220
By November those in heavy labour were getting two hundred and fifty grams a day and everybody else a hundred and twenty-five.

119
00:11:51.220 --> 00:12:00.470
What is more, there was less and less flour in that bread; cellulose, bran waste, linseed cake, whatever could be found went into the dough.

120
00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:03.800
In December the temperature fell below minus thirty.

121
00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:09.370
Water pipes burst, the electricity went out, the trams froze where they stood.

122
00:12:09.370 --> 00:12:14.460
People began carrying water by the bucket from holes cut in the ice of the river Neva.

123
00:12:14.460 --> 00:12:19.230
The building on Saint Isaac's Square entered that winter unprepared.

124
00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:26.080
The glass had long since gone with the pressure of the bombardments; they closed the windows with plywood and boards.

125
00:12:26.080 --> 00:12:28.930
There was no heating.

126
00:12:28.930 --> 00:12:31.780
Breath fogged in the corridors.

127
00:12:31.780 --> 00:12:42.630
The staff moved the most fragile parts of the collection into the inner rooms of the building, to the points furthest from any window; they raised the boxes off the floor and locked them into metal cabinets.

128
00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:46.920
Because cold and damp were not the only enemies of the seed.

129
00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:52.650
As the city's every store of food ran out, the rats got everywhere with an unbelievable hunger.

130
00:12:52.650 --> 00:12:58.540
The people at the institute saw the rats knocking boxes over, gnawing through paper envelopes.

131
00:12:58.540 --> 00:13:07.310
They tied the boxes with iron bars, insulated the feet of the cabinets, and in some rooms spent the night banging on tins to keep the rats away with the noise.

132
00:13:07.310 --> 00:13:09.680
And there were human beings.

133
00:13:09.680 --> 00:13:13.810
In that city hunger had turned every door into a possibility.

134
00:13:13.810 --> 00:13:20.020
In the institute's stores there was rice, wheat, corn, peas, lentils, almonds.

135
00:13:20.020 --> 00:13:27.190
A heap of food that today could be measured in tons, sitting in locked rooms, in an order known only to the botanists.

136
00:13:27.190 --> 00:13:30.360
The staff set up a system of watches.

137
00:13:30.360 --> 00:13:35.610
Rooms were kept locked, the keys were divided up, nobody went into a store alone.

138
00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:42.780
Night watches were stood in pairs; both for security and so that the person on watch would not freeze and fall asleep.

139
00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:50.590
When the air raid sirens sounded, it was also their job to climb to the roof of the building and put out incendiary bombs with sand.

140
00:13:50.590 --> 00:13:55.040
One fire would have meant twenty years of expeditions turned to ash.

141
00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:59.810
They were not the only side that knew what the collection was worth, either.

142
00:13:59.810 --> 00:14:07.300
The German agricultural administration had been interested in the genetic material at Soviet research stations from the very beginning;

143
00:14:07.300 --> 00:14:19.430
in the summer of nineteen forty-three a collecting detachment formed within the SS toured the research stations of occupied Ukraine and Crimea with two lorries and gathered up a portion of the samples Vavilov had spread across the country.

144
00:14:19.430 --> 00:14:23.240
They never reached the main collection in Leningrad.

145
00:14:23.240 --> 00:14:28.810
The city was besieged, bombed, left to starve; but it did not fall.

146
00:14:28.810 --> 00:14:38.540
And so the largest seed library in the world stayed for eight hundred and seventy-two days inside a building that was wasting away at the same rate as the body heat of the people protecting it.

147
00:14:38.540 --> 00:14:43.630
January and February of nineteen forty-two were the darkest months of the siege.

148
00:14:43.630 --> 00:14:52.080
Thousands of people were dying in the city every day; bodies were carried on sledges, could not be buried, and often waited on street corners.

149
00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:58.770
The number who died of hunger over the course of the siege is most commonly estimated at around seven hundred thousand.

150
00:14:58.770 --> 00:15:02.100
The people of the Plant Institute were among that number.

151
00:15:02.100 --> 00:15:09.590
Dmitri Ivanov, the botanist responsible for the rice collection, worked among tens of thousands of packets of rice.

152
00:15:09.590 --> 00:15:19.160
Most of them had come from Vavilov's Asian expeditions; each packet was the record of a separate village, a separate water regime, a separate ripening time.

153
00:15:19.160 --> 00:15:26.490
Ivanov spent that winter protecting these packets, and in the winter of nineteen forty-two he died of hunger in his own room.

154
00:15:26.490 --> 00:15:30.940
When he was found dead, thousands of packets of rice stood around him.

155
00:15:30.940 --> 00:15:36.830
Alexander Shchukin, the groundnut specialist, was found at his desk.

156
00:15:36.830 --> 00:15:40.480
In his hand was a packet from his own collection.

157
00:15:40.480 --> 00:15:46.210
Groundnuts were among the oiliest material in that building, the one carrying the densest calories.

158
00:15:46.210 --> 00:15:50.340
Shchukin had not opened the packet.

159
00:15:50.340 --> 00:15:54.950
Georgi Kreyer, the head of the medicinal plants department, died.

160
00:15:54.950 --> 00:15:58.920
Liliya Rodina, the oat specialist, died.

161
00:15:58.920 --> 00:16:04.490
In the institute's own account these deaths were for many years spoken of as nine people;

162
00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:11.980
more recent work based on the archives shows that the number of staff who died of hunger over the course of the siege was more than twice that.

163
00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:14.670
None of these deaths was a suicide.

164
00:16:14.670 --> 00:16:18.000
None of them was a theatrical gesture, either.

165
00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:28.530
Nobody at the institute had bound anybody with an oath; according to what the survivors said afterwards, no such ceremony, no such scene of swearing, ever took place.

166
00:16:28.530 --> 00:16:40.180
Only, everybody knew the same thing: if those seeds were eaten, one person would be full for one meal that day, and a variety that was the result of centuries of selection would be wiped off the earth.

167
00:16:40.180 --> 00:16:48.790
Among them were people who understood better than anyone what agriculture actually is; they knew how a variety is lost and how it never comes back.

168
00:16:48.790 --> 00:16:51.480
The war was going to end.

169
00:16:51.480 --> 00:16:54.970
The fields of the country were going to be sown again.

170
00:16:54.970 --> 00:16:58.620
What would be sown in those fields was in this building.

171
00:16:58.620 --> 00:17:05.470
One of the survivors, describing that winter, said that the hardest part of hunger was not being unable to eat.

172
00:17:05.470 --> 00:17:08.800
The hardest part was getting up in the morning.

173
00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:18.370
Lifting yourself out of bed, putting on your coat, walking down the dark corridor and opening the lock, counting the boxes, writing that day's note in the register.

174
00:17:18.370 --> 00:17:21.220
Every day, again.

175
00:17:21.220 --> 00:17:30.950
At the very time these people were dying, the founder of their institution was dying of the same cause twelve hundred kilometres to the south-east, in the prison at Saratov.

176
00:17:30.950 --> 00:17:43.400
Vavilov grew steadily weaker through nineteen forty-two; in prison conditions he went into a collapse brought on by malnutrition and died on the twenty-sixth of January, nineteen forty-three, at the age of fifty-five.

177
00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:48.970
The cause of death, in the medical term that appears in the records, was dystrophy.

178
00:17:48.970 --> 00:17:51.340
That is, hunger.

179
00:17:51.340 --> 00:17:56.750
The man who had travelled sixty-four countries to save the world from famine died of hunger.

180
00:17:56.750 --> 00:18:01.040
The people who protected the seeds he had collected died of hunger too.

181
00:18:01.040 --> 00:18:06.930
There was only one difference between them: the ones in Leningrad had food, and did not eat it.

182
00:18:06.930 --> 00:18:10.420
Vavilov never learned what his colleagues had done.

183
00:18:10.420 --> 00:18:14.550
They, in turn, did not know that their director was already dead.

184
00:18:14.550 --> 00:18:25.240
The news of his death reached his family only years later, and the true location of his grave was never fully disclosed; he is accepted to have been buried in a common cemetery in Saratov.

185
00:18:25.240 --> 00:18:33.370
In the spring of nineteen forty-two, as the siege entered its second year, those still standing at the institute took on an impossible piece of work.

186
00:18:33.370 --> 00:18:37.820
The problem was this: a potato cannot be stored like a seed.

187
00:18:37.820 --> 00:18:43.070
A tuber is living tissue; within a few months it either rots or sprouts itself away.

188
00:18:43.070 --> 00:18:48.640
The only way to keep the thousands of potato varieties in the collection alive was to put them in the soil.

189
00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:54.050
And the soil was outside the city, and outside the city was the front.

190
00:18:54.050 --> 00:19:04.260
Vadim Lekhnovich, the man in charge of the potato collection, and his wife and colleague Olga Voskresenskaya kept the tubers alive through the winter in the most suitable room in the building,

191
00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:09.030
regulating the temperature by hand and hauling the fuel for the stove themselves.

192
00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:15.080
In spring they loaded the tubers into rucksacks and handcarts and went out to the fields on the outskirts of the city.

193
00:19:15.080 --> 00:19:18.090
Fields within artillery range.

194
00:19:18.090 --> 00:19:23.340
Part of the planting was done between rounds of shellfire, in windows of a few hours a day.

195
00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:28.910
They tended the crop through the summer, lifted it in the autumn, stored it again for the winter.

196
00:19:28.910 --> 00:19:32.560
They repeated this every year that the siege lasted.

197
00:19:32.560 --> 00:19:37.810
On the twenty-seventh of January, nineteen forty-four, the siege was broken.

198
00:19:37.810 --> 00:19:44.660
That evening, for the first time in three years, what stood over the city was not bombardment but celebration fire.

199
00:19:44.660 --> 00:19:51.350
When the inventory was taken at the institute, the picture that emerged was this: the collection was where it should be.

200
00:19:51.350 --> 00:19:55.640
It had not been eaten, not been stolen, not been burned.

201
00:19:55.640 --> 00:20:03.610
The registers had been kept complete right through the war; which box had been moved where, which sample had been renewed and when, was all written down.

202
00:20:03.610 --> 00:20:10.140
Part of the seed had lost its power to germinate over the long cold years and had to be rescued by sowing.

203
00:20:10.140 --> 00:20:20.990
The years that followed went into exactly that; in a country coming out of a war, with burned stations and missing staff, the collection was returned to the soil sample by sample and renewed.

204
00:20:20.990 --> 00:20:27.040
But the story did not end there, and what came next was not the ending the people in that building deserved.

205
00:20:27.040 --> 00:20:31.650
In nineteen forty-eight Lysenko won his final victory.

206
00:20:31.650 --> 00:20:42.500
At the August session of the academy of agricultural sciences, genetics was formally declared a false science; defending chromosomal heredity stopped being an opinion and became an offence.

207
00:20:42.500 --> 00:20:49.670
Courses were removed from the universities, laboratories were closed, hundreds of researchers were put out of their jobs.

208
00:20:49.670 --> 00:20:55.400
The Plant Institute took its share; the generation Vavilov had raised was dispersed.

209
00:20:55.400 --> 00:21:00.650
The institution that had guarded the collection through starvation was pruned back in peacetime.

210
00:21:00.650 --> 00:21:07.660
The price Soviet agriculture paid in this period would be discussed at length in the harvest arguments of the decades that followed.

211
00:21:07.660 --> 00:21:13.710
Lysenko's grip only began to break from the middle of the nineteen fifties, after Stalin's death.

212
00:21:13.710 --> 00:21:19.760
Vavilov was formally rehabilitated in nineteen fifty-five; all the charges were dropped.

213
00:21:19.760 --> 00:21:23.890
His name slowly came back into the publications.

214
00:21:23.890 --> 00:21:28.500
In nineteen sixty-eight the institute in Leningrad was named after him.

215
00:21:28.500 --> 00:21:33.750
Today the world knows that institution simply as the Vavilov Institute.

216
00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:36.920
The collection, for its part, went on growing.

217
00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:46.010
Today it holds more than three hundred and thirty thousand samples, and among them are varieties found nowhere else on earth, whose wild relatives disappeared long ago.

218
00:21:46.010 --> 00:21:56.380
In twenty ten a part of this inheritance came under threat once again: the land of the Pavlovsk experimental station south of Leningrad was put up for sale for housing construction.

219
00:21:56.380 --> 00:22:06.590
That was where the collection of fruit and soft fruit was held, the collection that can only be kept alive as living plants; thousands of trees and bushes that cannot be put in a crate and moved.

220
00:22:06.590 --> 00:22:14.240
Scientists from all over the world started a campaign, the matter was carried into the international press, and the sale process was stopped.

221
00:22:14.240 --> 00:22:18.850
Vavilov's idea itself is today buried in the ice of the Arctic.

222
00:22:18.850 --> 00:22:31.140
The global seed vault opened in twenty and eight in Norway's Svalbard archipelago is precisely the modern form of what he imagined: a shelter carved into frozen rock, holding the backup of every gene bank in the world.

223
00:22:31.140 --> 00:22:37.030
Among the more than a million samples in the vault are boxes sent from the Vavilov Institute.

224
00:22:37.030 --> 00:22:48.200
Which means that the descendants of those same varieties, protected in Leningrad for eight hundred and seventy-two days from rats, from cold, from bombs and from hunger, now lie hidden inside the Arctic.

225
00:22:48.200 --> 00:22:52.010
The real business of the seed library was never the past.

226
00:22:52.010 --> 00:22:56.460
Nobody collected a mountain wheat in Ethiopia as a keepsake.

227
00:22:56.460 --> 00:23:05.230
Those seeds were the carriers of the answer to a disease that has not yet appeared, a drought that has not yet happened, a problem nobody has yet given a name to.

228
00:23:05.230 --> 00:23:14.160
Today, breeders searching for new varieties under climate change are looking at the very samples that were brought down from a mountain on the back of a mule a century ago.

229
00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:24.050
In the winter of nineteen forty-two, in that cold building on Saint Isaac's Square, the man who died without opening the packets of rice in front of him could not have known this.

230
00:23:24.050 --> 00:23:26.740
But it was for exactly this that he died.

231
00:23:26.740 --> 00:23:30.230
Nöron History'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

232
00:23:30.230 --> 00:23:34.040
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
